# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Line endings. This file is not cosmetic — it is load-bearing on Windows.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites checked-out
# text files with CRLF endings. A shell script with CRLF fails immediately:
#
#     ./publish.sh: /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# and worse, a pre-commit hook with CRLF fails silently in some setups — the
# guard appears installed and blocks nothing. Forcing LF removes the whole
# class of problem.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

*               text=auto eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
.githooks/*     text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.jsx           text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf

# Binary, never touched
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.webp   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webm   binary
*.mp4    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.pdf    binary
